#!bovnar 1.1
# om.bvnr — single source of truth for the OM 2 unit profile.
#
# HOW THIS WORKS
#   Hand-edited. gen_profiles.py (at the repo root) regenerates the C profile
#   tables from this file on every build; never edit the generated *.gen.{h,inc}.
#
# WHAT THIS VOCABULARY IS
#   OM 2, the Ontology of units of Measure (Rijgersberg, van Assem, Top;
#   Wageningen). An OWL ontology whose units are IRIs under
#   http://www.ontology-of-units-of-measure.org/resource/om-2/, and this profile
#   spells them by their LOCAL NAME -- the part after the last '/'. OM is the
#   second unit ontology of the semantic web, beside QUDT: where QUDT is the
#   vocabulary of digital twins and building semantics, OM is the one agrifood,
#   food science, life-cycle assessment and FAIR/ELN data reach for, and it is
#   what Wikidata's unit items most often align to.
#
#   A FLAT profile. An OM local name is ONE WHOLE TOKEN: "kilogramPerCubicMetre"
#   is matched entire. Its structure is not parsed here, although OM states one
#   -- see the next section, which is why it is not.
#
# EVERY TARGET IN THIS FILE WAS DERIVED FROM OM'S OWN STRUCTURE
#   OM does not merely name its units, it COMPOSES them: a PrefixedUnit states
#   its prefix and its base, a UnitDivision its numerator and denominator, a
#   UnitMultiplication its two terms, a UnitExponentiation its base and exponent,
#   and a SingularUnit its factor against the unit it is defined from. So the
#   `.bovnar` target of a compound row is not a reading of the local name: it is
#   built from the same composition, bottom-up, out of the atoms this file maps
#   by hand -- which is why "gramPerPetalitre" says "g/P~L" and cannot say
#   anything else.
#
#   Each row was then checked against OM: the derived target is parsed by the
#   library and its factor and dimension vector compared with what the ontology
#   itself resolves the unit to. check_profile_factors.py runs that comparison
#   as a test, so it stays true rather than having been true once.
#
# FULL IRIs ARE NOT ACCEPTED
#   "om:kilogram", never "om:http://www.ontology-of-units-of-measure.org/...".
#   Same rule, and the same reason, as qudt.bvnr: a unit slot is at most 255
#   bytes, a ':' inside it already means something here, and the local name is
#   the identifying part.
#
# WHERE OM AND BOVNAR DISAGREE, THE ROW IS ABSENT RATHER THAN APPROXIMATE
#   OM's `year` is the GREGORIAN year, 31 556 952 s; native `yr` is the Julian
#   31 557 600 s. Twenty-one parts per million apart, dimensionally identical,
#   and mapping one to the other would have been invisible in every test that
#   does not check the number. It is refused below, and so is everything OM
#   builds on it -- `gigayear`, `reciprocalYear`, `cubicMetrePerYear`. The same
#   rule keeps OM's unqualified `calorie-*` and `BritishThermalUnit-*` out: OM
#   states no factor for those, and a unit whose magnitude the publisher does
#   not state is not one this table can carry.
#
# THE ARBITRARY UNITS ARE NOT HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE
#   OM has an InternationalUnit, a colonyFormingUnit, an amylaseUnit. UCUM has
#   them too, and bovnar already carries UCUM's as OPAQUE base units with ids of
#   their own (doc/11 section 7.1). Giving OM a second identity for the same
#   assay unit would make om:InternationalUnit and ucum:[IU] incommensurable
#   with each other -- two units that cannot be compared although they are the
#   same unit. They are refused here instead, which costs an error message and
#   not a wrong comparison.
#
# THIS PROFILE IS READ AND WRITTEN
#   Unlike cf.bvnr, an OM code names a UNIT, so bvn_unit_to_profile("om", u) has
#   an answer. Where several local names are worth the same native unit, exactly
#   one keeps the reverse spelling and the rest carry `.reverse = false`. Shape
#   decides it before length does: a prefixed native target takes the name OM
#   builds the same way, so 10 A is written `decaampere` and not the shorter,
#   stranger `biot`, and an unprefixed one is never written as a PrefixedUnit.
#   Composed names lose to atom-shaped ones outright -- OM's
#   "cubicMetrePerSquareMetre" is worth a metre, and a metre must not be written
#   back as one.
#
# SOURCE, VERSION AND LICENSING
#   Vocabulary  OM 2.0 (om-2.0.rdf)
#   Verified    against that publication, retrieved 2026-07-30 from
#               https://github.com/HajoRijgersberg/OM
#   Publisher   (c) 2005-2025 Hajo Rijgersberg, Don Willems, Jan Top,
#               Wageningen University and Research Centre, The Netherlands
#   Licence     Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0),
#               https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ -- the authors do
#               not endorse this project
#   Taken       unit local names, and the compositions (prefix/base,
#               numerator/denominator, term/term, base/exponent) each target
#               above was derived from. No OM label, description, annotation or
#               factor text
#   Modified    yes -- a subset, mapped onto bovnar's native unit registry
#
#   OM is published by an EEA maker, so the EU database right applies alongside
#   copyright; CC BY 4.0 section 2(a)(1) licences it expressly, which is what
#   makes this extraction covered rather than merely small. The attribution the
#   licence requires lives in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md, in full.

.schema_version = <uint:16> 1;

